⟦400⟧{{smaller block|If in fig. 6 the two pictures L and R are interchanged in both pictures (<math>a</math> or <math>b</math> in fig. 11), then the image-points for H are closer together than those for V; thus in stereoscopic vision H appears in front of P, and V behind it. No change is made to the relief by turning the picture upside down (<math>c</math> and <math>d</math> in fig. 11). In fig. 11<math>d</math>, the pictures are in the same positions as when the photographs are taken (F<sub>1</sub>, F<sub>2</sub> in fig. 6). Obviously transparent pictures can be easily reversed; in other cases it must be effected by mirrors (Wheatstone, Dove and others) or by an erecting reflection prism. The original unbroken plate (fig. 11<math>d</math>) can be seen in the pseudo-stereoscope
[[FIGURE]]
